% ============================================================================
% precipitation-minimum-diameter — for the precipitation types whose NWS
% definition states one, the MINIMUM diameter in millimeters a particle must
% exceed to count as that type (adj-facts-stdlib, METEOROLOGY).
% ============================================================================
% A sibling to the already-shipped `precipitation-types.adj`
% (`precip_form(precip, form)`, ONE defining physical form per precipitation
% type): a new `precipitation_min_diameter(precip, min_diameter_mm)` table
% names the numeric diameter THRESHOLD the SAME NOAA National Weather
% Service Glossary states for a type, decoded from spans already sitting
% unused inside `precipitation-types.adj`'s own provenance block — no new
% WebFetch. That table's `form` column has room for only ONE descriptive
% word per type; two of the SAME already-quoted NWS Glossary sentences also
% carry a minimum-size number that never made it into a row:
%
%   rain → 0.5
%     "Precipitation that falls to earth in drops more than 0.5 mm in
%      diameter."
%   hail → 5
%     "Showery precipitation in the form of irregular pellets or balls of
%      ice more than 5 mm in diameter, falling from a cumulonimbus cloud."
%
% Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? precipitation_min_diameter(hail, $MinDiameterMm)      % 5
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5, a TWO-column table like
% `precipitation-types.adj`'s own `precip_form`): each `row` lowers to a
% ground relation `precipitation_min_diameter(precip, min_diameter_mm)`
% carrying the citation, so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding
% query — the engine returns the threshold WITH its source, on the CPU,
% with zero model calls, and abstains on any precipitation type the cited
% NWS Glossary spans do not give a diameter figure for (`snow`, `sleet`,
% and `freezing_rain`, whose own already-cited spans in
% `precipitation-types.adj` describe HOW the water forms/freezes, not a
% minimum particle size).
%
% Only TWO of `precipitation-types.adj`'s five precipitation types have a
% diameter figure in the ALREADY-cited spans; this table is deliberately
% narrow rather than inventing a threshold the source does not state. The
% query also runs BACKWARD — bind a diameter and recall which type it
% names:
%
%     ? precipitation_min_diameter($Precip, 0.5)   % rain
%
% Provenance: reproduces, byte-for-byte, the SAME rain span already quoted
% inside `precipitation-types.adj`'s own table-level provenance envelope,
% from the SAME NOAA National Weather Service Glossary that table already
% cites — no new WebFetch, only a different schema decoding a part of each
% already-verified quote. An ADJ `table` carries ONE provenance envelope
% (per `precipitation-types.adj`'s own convention), so `source`/`locator`/
% `trust` below hold the rain span; the hail row's own span is listed above
% for independent auditability. `trust authoritative` — the same tier
% `precipitation-types.adj` already carries (NOAA NWS, a primary U.S.
% government source).
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table precipitation_min_diameter {
    columns precip, min_diameter_mm

    row (rain, 0.5)
    row (hail, 5)

    source "Precipitation that falls to earth in drops more than 0.5 mm in diameter."
    locator "https://forecast.weather.gov/glossary.php?word=RAIN"
    trust authoritative
}
